  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe core idea is to use an iterative greedy algorithm that focuses on reducing the worst-case overlap for a specific lag at each iteration. This approach avoids the computational expense of evaluating all lags at every step, instead concentrating on improving the most problematic overlap. The bottleneck addressed is the high computational cost of full correlation evaluation during optimization. By iteratively identifying and minimizing the maximum overlap, this method reduces the effective search space and leverages local refinement to achieve better convergence. The strategy balances exploration and exploitation by occasionally perturbing the solution to escape local minima while maintaining the integral constraint through projection.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe represent h as a vector h \u2208 [0,1]\u207f with \u2211h[i] = n/2, where n is the number of samples over [0,2]. The overlap for a specific lag k_max is given by:\nC_k_max = \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) (1 - h(x + k_max)) dx\nThe objective is to minimize the maximum overlap over all lags, which is equivalent to minimizing:\nC\u2085 = max_k \u222b h(x) (1 - h(x + k)) dx\nFor a given k_max, the overlap integral becomes:\nC_k_max = 1 - \u222b h(x) h(x + k_max) dx\nThe optimization subproblem for a fixed k_max is to minimize this value while maintaining the integral constraint. This allows us to use SLSQP for constrained optimization, with the equality constraint \u2211h[i] = n/2, and bounds [0,1] on each h[i]. The worst-case lag is dynamically identified at each iteration using the current h.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStage 1: Parent validation and initialization\n- Verify initial_h_values meets \u2211h[i] = n/2 and 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1.\n- Initialize h using the provided parent or a regular pattern if none is available.\n\nStage 2: Core iteration loop\n- While budget remains and convergence criteria are unmet:\n   a. Compute full correlation matrix: corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")\n   b. Find k_max = argmax(corr * dx)\n   c. Extract the relevant subproblem for k_max:\n       i. Define objective function: -\u222b h(x) h(x + k_max) dx (since we minimize C_k_max)\n       ii. Formulate as an optimization problem with the integral constraint\n   d. Solve subproblem using SLSQP with bounds [0,1] and constraint \u2211h[i] = n/2\n   e. Update h with the optimal solution\n   f. Perturb h by adding small random noise and clipping to [0,1]\n   g. Project the perturbed solution to restore the integral constraint\n\nStage 3: Convergence and termination\n- Monitor improvement in max overlap. If improvements fall below 1e-6 for 5 consecutive iterations, terminate.\n- If budget is exhausted, return the current best h.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration:\n- n = 400 (balance accuracy and computational cost)\n- Initial guess: initial_h_values if available, else evenly distributed 1s\n- Perturbation amplitude: 0.05 (decays by 0.95 every 50 iterations)\n- SLSQP tolerances: 1e-8\n- Maximum iterations: 200\n\nVariations:\n- For n \u2264 300: Increase perturbation to 0.1 for better exploration\n- For n \u2265 500: Reduce perturbation to 0.03 to improve stability\n- When convergence stalls: Switch to random restarts with increased perturbation\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nDominant cost: O(n\u00b2) for correlation + O(n) for projection. With n=400, this is 160,000 operations per iteration. At 200 iterations, this equals 32 million operations. Python's numpy should handle this within 600 seconds.\n\nTermination criteria:\n- After 200 iterations of Stage 2\n- If budget is exhausted\n- If improvement falls below 1e-6 for 5 consecutive iterations\n\nRobustness measures:\n- Always enforce constraint via projection\n- Use initial guess as fallback if no valid solution is found\n- Implement numerical stability checks for all operations\n- Use diminishing perturbation amplitude over time\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:\n- Verify \u2211h[i] \u2248 n/2 with absolute tolerance 1e-6\n- Confirm 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- Recompute C\u2085 using the exact objective function\n\nSanity checks:\n- Validate initial guess meets constraints\n- Confirm projection maintains the sum\n- Ensure correlation computation uses correct dx = 2.0/n\n- Verify all operations preserve 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 bounds\n\nRe-evaluation protocol:\n- Recompute C\u2085 using the original objective function\n- Check for numerical stability in the correlation result\n- Confirm all operations match the task's exact metric\n- Compare with initial_h_values if no improvement is found",
